      "description": "Chalmers considers a sound F. The target does not assume soundness, so it strengthens item (b) to cover unsound theories as well. Soundness is not needed to prove the conditional."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The target fixes a specific setting that the passage leaves implicit: F is an arithmetic theory with a Δ₁ axiom presentation that proves every theorem of IΣ₁."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The consequent is G(F) itself, rather than a separate assertion of its truth. This is the usual disquotational reading of “G(F) is true” for this particular sentence."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target gets the scope of item (b) right: F proves the whole conditional, with its own consistency as antecedent and its own Gödel sentence as consequent. It does not say that F proves either one separately."
